     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order. Metformin 1700 mg three times daily is 5100 mg/day, which is about double the maximum daily dose. The labeled maximum is 2550 mg/day for immediate-release metformin and 2000 mg/day for extended-release. At 70 kg with normal renal function, the dose is not the renal issue. The problem is the total daily amount, and it could cause serious lactic acidosis or GI toxicity.\n\nSome possibilities for what was intended:\n- **850 mg three times daily** (2550 mg/day, the IR maximum), which is a common regimen.\n- **1700 mg once daily** with the evening meal, if extended-release. That is still above the 2000 mg/day ER maximum only if given with other doses, so it is within the limit.\n- **500 mg three times daily** or another lower dose, if this is a starting or titration dose.\n\nPlease confirm the intended dose, frequency, and formulation (IR or ER), and I'll enter the corrected order. If 5100 mg/day really is intended, please verify it with the prescriber or pharmacy before I proceed."
      }
